= Section A
{parent=2022}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

The emission mechanism is <Cherenkov radiation>. An <electron> moving faster than the <phase velocity> $c/n$ of <light> in the material excites <electric polarization> disturbances whose radiation adds coherently on a cone. This does not require the <electron> to exceed the <speed of light> in <vacuum>. Taking $n=1.4$ as constant over the detected optical band, the <Cherenkov cone angle> satisfies
$$
\cos\theta_C=\frac{c}{nv}=\frac1{n\beta},\qquad \beta=\frac vc.
$$
Emission requires $\beta>1/n$. The detector accepts $\theta_C<20^\circ$, so
$$
\frac1{n\beta}>\cos20^\circ,\qquad
\boxed{\frac1{1.4}<\beta<\frac1{1.4\cos20^\circ}.}
$$
Numerically,
$$
\boxed{0.71429c<v<0.76013c,\qquad
2.1414\times10^8<v<2.2788\times10^8\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The lower endpoint has vanishing <Cherenkov radiation> yield, and the upper endpoint is excluded by the stated strict angular acceptance. Faster <electrons> emit at larger cone angles: at $v\simeq c$, $\theta_C\simeq\cos^{-1}(1/1.4)=44.4^\circ$, outside this detector's acceptance. The angular condition gives the accepted speed interval conditional on rays reaching the detector; no collection geometry or total signal yield is specified.

= 2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <Johnson-Nyquist noise> formula gives the open-circuit mean-square <voltage> in a small <frequency> band $d\nu$. When a source <electrical resistance> $R$ drives a load $R$ with <impedance matching>, the load sees half the source <voltage>, so the available noise <power> is
$$
dP_t=\frac{\langle V^2\rangle/4}{R}=k_BT_t\,d\nu.
$$
The factor of four in the open-circuit formula is therefore not an extra factor in the delivered <power>.

The transmitter's <antenna gain> $G$ makes the axial spectral <energy flux> at range $d$
$$
\frac{dS}{d\nu}=\frac{G}{4\pi d^2}k_BT_t.
$$
Multiplication by the receiving <antenna effective area> $A$ gives $dP_r=k_BT_tGA\,d\nu/(4\pi d^2)$. Define the equivalent receiving <antenna temperature> $T_A$ by the available matched-load noise $dP_r=k_BT_A\,d\nu$. Then
$$
\boxed{T_A=T_t\frac{GA}{4\pi d^2}
=(10^4\,\mathrm K)\frac{10^5(1.8\,\mathrm{m^2})}{4\pi(10^3\,\mathrm m)^2}
=143.24\,\mathrm K\simeq143\,\mathrm K.}
$$
This is the transmitter-induced equivalent <noise temperature> of the receiver port. It does not determine the actual physical <temperature> of its internal <electrical resistance>, or any additional receiver and background noise. The calculation assumes the usual far-field link, negligible path loss beyond geometric spreading, matched <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> at the two antennas and the stated effective collecting area. No additional one-half polarization factor is needed for the matched polarization of radiation from one transmitting <antenna>.

= 3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Each mutually incoherent patch of an extended <star> produces a displaced <double-slit interference> pattern. The detector adds their <light intensities>. For two apertures separated by a projected <interferometric baseline> $D$, a source direction offset by a small <angle> $\vartheta_x$ changes their <phase difference> by $2\pi D\vartheta_x/\lambda$. The normalized sum over the source's angular brightness distribution is its <first-order optical coherence>, whose magnitude is the <interferometric visibility> for equally bright channels.

For a uniform circular stellar disk of full <angular diameter> $\alpha$, the normalized angular <Fourier transform> gives the <uniform-disk stellar visibility>
$$
\mathcal V(D)=\left|\frac{2J_1(x)}x\right|,\qquad
x=\frac{\pi D\alpha}{\lambda}.
$$
Here $J_1$ is the order-one <Bessel function of the first kind>. This follows by integrating $e^{i(2\pi D/\lambda)\vartheta_x}$ over the disk and dividing by its area. In particular, its <power series> gives $2J_1(x)/x=1-x^2/8+O(x^4)$.

For an ordinary slit separation $d=1\,\mathrm{mm}$, using the paper's $\alpha=22.6\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{rad}$ and observing <wavelength> $\lambda=570\,\mathrm{nm}$,
$$
\frac{d\alpha}{\lambda}=3.965\times10^{-4},\qquad
x=1.2456\times10^{-3},\qquad
1-\mathcal V\simeq1.94\times10^{-7}.
$$
Even $d=10\,\mathrm{mm}$ gives a visibility loss of only $1.94\times10^{-5}$. Thus millimetre separation leaves the <interference fringes> almost indistinguishable from those of a point source; it gives very little sensitivity to the diameter.

The movable mirrors in the <Michelson stellar interferometer> collect light at a large adjustable separation $D$ and redirect it into closely spaced output beams. They increase the sampling <interferometric baseline> independently of the fixed output slit spacing $d$: $D$ sets sensitivity to source structure, whereas the output optics set the screen's <interference fringe spacing>. The first <Bessel function> zero is $x_1=3.83170597$, hence
$$
\boxed{D_{\rm null}=\frac{x_1}{\pi}\frac\lambda\alpha
=1.21967\frac{570\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm m}{22.6\times10^{-8}}
=3.076\,\mathrm m\simeq3.08\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The <interference fringes> disappear at this baseline even though each beam separately remains bright.

\Image[2022-q3-stellar-visibility.png]
{title=Original calculated <interferometric visibility> for the given stellar diameter and <wavelength>}

The value $3.08\,\mathrm m$ assumes a uniform disk. The original question supplies a diameter without a <surface brightness> law; real <limb darkening> changes the precise visibility zero. A one-dimensional uniformly bright strip of the same full angular width instead has a <sinc function> visibility and first zero $D=\lambda/\alpha=2.522\,\mathrm m$. That is a different source model, not the circular-disk result. The robust conclusion without a <surface brightness> model is that a baseline of a few metres, rather than millimetres, is required.

